what is  [Music]  moving away from its RPG roots the  original of the city of Final Fantasy  traded turn-based battles for real-time  action duels featuring an all-star cast  from the franchise it told an original  story that celebrated the series's  diverse incarnations while also  presenting an odd yet satisfying  approach to character action but in  aiming for a more competitive focus  along with some half-hearted offerings  for solo content the newest installment  Dissidia Final Fantasy NT from Square  Enix and team ninja loses sight of what  makes the Final Fantasy series so  memorable resulting in a hollow journey  through a franchise's storied history  squall wait what is it is it another  summon when the war in God's materia and  spiritists call forth the heroes and  villains to engage in a new fight for  the fate of their collective universes  the Warriors soon realized that there is  a greater threat lurking in the  background  forcing rivals to set aside their  differences to take on the encroaching  menace much like its predecessors  Dissidia NT is very much a celebration  of the Final Fantasy mythos set across  different locales from this series is  past including Final Fantasy once  Cornelia and sevens Midgar several the  stages recreate the same style and tone  found from their respective titles along  with the return of iconic musical themes  and other references to past titles  including the appearance of the tutorial  mukou who continues to overstay his  welcome the brawler pays great attention  to creating a mash-up of the most iconic  elements from 30 years worth of  noteworthy games Dissidia NT story  campaign makes attempts to justify the  backdrop of its chaotic slugfest while  offering some room for fanservice taking  control of several heroes from the Final  Fantasy series they'll fight against  their own rivals and corrupted copies of  other characters as they come to grips  with the reality of their present battle  well the many cutscenes throughout are  charming the story as a whole feels  undercooked even disregarding some of  the newcomers to the roster this is made  worse by some rather odd choices and how  you go about experiencing the narrative  story progression uses a node-based  system with cutscenes and key battles  costing memoria to unlock you'll often  have to grind experience in the games  other modes earn more tokens for the  campaign this ultimately makes the  campaign a fragmented experience you  can't go through at your own pace  weakening the impact of the story's more  meaningful encounters often building up  dramatic fights that you've already  experienced several times over how did I  know you would be here to switch things  up during the story your party will  engage in boss battles against several  of the game's summoned monsters but  while the scale of these fights are  impressive and the game's visual luster  shines throughout these battles also  present massive difficulty spikes in the  campaign given that they're capable of  wiping out your party in single moves  with ease these large battles feel more  like clumsy exercises and trial and  error rather than a culmination of your  skill with a roster of well over twenty  characters including familiar faces such  as the brooding loner types Cloud Strife  &amp; squall Leonhart along with newcomers  ramza and noctus from Final Fantasy  Tactics and Final Fantasy 15  respectively there's an impressive set  of brawlers to choose from each with  their own unique play styles  the city is action will force you into  split-second decisions to make sure your  hits count by building up attack power  with non-lethal brave attacks draining  your opponent's power in the process  you'll be able to deal greater amounts  of raw damage to your opponents with  lethal HP attacks  moreover collecting energy from randomly  placed crystals will allow your team to  call in your chosen summon monster  turning the flow of battle in your favor  battles are set in large arenas so  you'll constantly be on the move  utilizing defensive maneuvers along with  support spells and buffs to stay one  step ahead of your rivals combat is  mostly kept on a relentless pace and  when you're in the thick of it the  fighting system allows players to  exhibit some rather clever strategies  rewarding those who can read their foes  next move while the system may come off  as a bit overwhelming for newcomers the  generous tutorial mode will put you  through your paces to learn all the  necessary mechanics to survive  [Music]  the three-on-three format makes for more  hectic battles and while these are fun  to take part in for most of the times  they often result in overly busy  encounters with all fighters bunching up  made slightly worse by an overstuffed  user interface the camera also struggles  to keep up with the action which is  especially frustrating on the higher  difficulties er during online play as  you clear through battles increase your  player rank and character level require  Gil for use in the item shop while you  can buy cosmetics and collectibles in  game you can also find treasure packages  yielding a set of randomized rewards the  rest assured you can acquire every item  on your own as there are no real money  microtransactions in the game whatsoever  [Music]  the city antes online experience is a  big focus for the game including ranked  matches along with custom games tuned to  your own preferences you'll be up  against skilled players who are looking  to boost the rep online unfortunately  simply waiting to take part in online  battles can be a chore during the first  week of the game's release online  matchmaking took upwards to 3 to 5  minutes to get a game going moreover  several the matches were burdened with  sudden lag spikes and skipping resulting  in some rather uneven encounters what's  most disappointing is that in going and  headstrong into its competitive  multiplayer angle Dissidia NT doesn't  offer much outside of it if the online  multiplayer and repeat excursions into  the gauntlet boat don't interest you in  the long term then you may find yourself  with few reasons to proceed with the  games already repetitive offerings for  all its attempts to honor Square Enix's  long-running series the city of Final  Fantasy NT stumbles far too often when  trying to replicate the heart of the  series it's paying tribute to while that  manages to offer fun and responsive  combat along with an infectious charm  throughout it struggles to advance much  from the previous Dissidia titles with  the story that sped piecemeal behind  arbitrary gating several combat  encounters that feel out of place and  unreliable online systems that don't  function when you need them to this  online brawler is enabled to live up to  the series as steadfastly tries to  celebrate  [Music]  please make it interesting  you

